Z||000004||sloth85GT||08-07-2001||02:59 AM||||Headliners most often have to completely be replaced. You can't just glue them back on because of the board that's underneath. It is like gluing (sp?) your hand to the beach. Not gonna happen. Sure it'll be a fine short term fix, but guaranteed it'll fall again. It's a common problem amongst Fieros. Mine is hanging there as we speak until I get around to buying a headliner kit. I just have two button pins holding it in right above where people heads are.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.mts.net/~kuzma/fieronl.gif"> <BR>Dan<BR>1985 Silver GT||||reg||1
Z||000005||Pontiaddict||08-07-2001||03:06 AM||||Mine just fell while it was sitting waiting for new shift cables.<P>I swear, when I fix the starter, the motor is gonna blow up on me.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Carrolles||08-07-2001||08:43 AM||||The headliner fabric is foam backed.  With age the foam deterioriates to dust and lets the fabric fall from the backing board (headliner board) which looks like pressed fiberglass mat.<P>To properly repair this you should completely remove the headliner board, peel off the fabric, scrape off (I use a wisk broom) the remnants of the foam, and glue new fabric back on.<P>Several people have coated the headliner board with resin or some other coating (do a search on headliner) prior to recovering.<P>I get my replacement foam backed headliner fabric at a local fabric store for around $7.50/yard.  I usually get about 1-1/2 yards to have extra to do the sunvisors but I think a yard might be just enough.  I use 3m Foamfast74 Spray glue made for foam backed fabrics.  It sprays on like silly string and does not wet the fabric.  Spray it on the back of the fabric as well as on the headliner board.  Then place the fabric on the board.  You'll have about 10 minutes to work out wrinkles etc before the glue sets.<P>Many people say that spray glue does not work.  My experience over the past 20 years is different using the 3m Foamfast74.  Don't use generic spray adhesive, it will let go in a day or two.<P>You can also use contact glue brushed onto the board (I'm not sure if you also apply it to the fabric).  I've never tried contact glue but several on this board have had good results with it.<P>As a temporary repair you can use upholstry twist pins to hold the sagging fabric to the headliner board.  I get them at WalMart in the fabric department.  They look like buttons with corkscrew shaped pins about the size of a push pin.  Simply screw them through the fabric into the headliner board.  If you place them in a decorative pattern it will resemble tuck and rolled fabric.  You can also spray paint the heads (the button looking part) to match the fabric to make them less noticable.<P>To remove the headliner from the car you'll need to remove the trim, coat hooks, sunvisors, map light, and sunroof trim if so equipped.<P>The trim along the bottom of the door opening is held on by several small hex head screws located under metal push in hole covers.  The covers can be pried out with a small screwdriver.<P>The trim along the top of the window opening is held on by plastic retainers which almost always break when pried off.  You can get replacements at parts stores.<P>The big trim piece that runs from floor to ceiling behind the doors is held on by the seatbelt bolt, a screw in the door jam, and a plastic retainer clip near the seatbelt bolt (all this is from a foggy memory).  When re-installing this piece be careful.  There is usually black gook along the top edge that will get all over the new headliner fabric when you jam it back in position.  Clean it off real good first.<P>It's not a hard job to do and the results can really dress up the looks of the interior.  <P>Good luck,<P>Carroll||||reg||1
Z||000007||rogergarrison||08-07-2001||01:03 PM||||Im one who recommends contact glue like they sell at hardware stores for a couple dollars a pint for like linoleum countertops. You can brush, roll, or spray it with a spray gun (dont thin it, spray it rite out of can, sprays out in stringy pattern too). coat board and thin coat on fabric too. It sticks immediatly on contact, so make sure you have it placed correctly and no wrinkles before it touches. It absolutely will never come back down unless you leave your car under water for 5 years. I just redid mine in naugahyde over the fabric i did 3 years ago. It wouldnt even come loose in a small area, so i just left it on for padding. ||||reg||1
