Z||000011||Lisa 86 2m4||08-08-2001||05:10 PM||||My quick fix (since about 2 1/2 years ago, someday I'll get around to replacing the liner) was to get out my trusty staple gun. I have gray interior and surprisingly you hardly notice the silver staples....<BR>But I have recently toyed with the idea, maybe someone can advise on the feasiblity of it....coating the board with the fiberglass matting and resin, then as it's tacky placing over it gray vinyl for a leather look. Any comments?<BR>Lisa||||reg||1
Z||000012||StuGood||08-08-2001||05:52 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Lisa 86 2m4:<BR><B>....coating the board with the fiberglass matting and resin, then as it's tacky placing over it gray vinyl for a leather look. Any comments?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>There's nothing wrong with using vinyl as far as I know.  But I would strongly (pun intended) recommend you let the resin cure completely and air out thoroughly, before you apply adhesive and install it the car, or it will "fume" you out of the car.  It smells nasty.  <P>When I re-covered my headliner, I put polyester fiberglass resin on, let it cure overnight and put it outside in the sun for a day, just to make sure all the smell the was baked out.  Then I applied glue and fabric.  ||||reg||4
Z||000013||Lisa 86 2m4||08-08-2001||06:48 PM||||I was kind of envisioning using the tackiness of the fiberglass as the adhesive. You think?||||reg||1
